          "type": "turn",
          "question": "How do I know whether a three-character category can stand on its own as a usable code?",
          "answer": "Three-character codes in ICD-10-CM represent categories of diseases or conditions. Each three-character code can stand alone as a valid code, but their usage may depend on whether there are additional details available in a Clinical context. \n\nAccording to the submission from the source titled \"ICD-10-CM code structure and the conventions that govern selection,\" three-character forms are the main categories. They do not have a decimal point if they stand alone, for example, \"I10\" for hypertension. However, if the condition requires more specificity, coded data may extend to four or more characters, such as \"E11.9,\" where additional characters provide details about the cause, anatomical site, or severity of the condition.\n\nIn summary, a three-character category can stand alone when it captures a specific condition without needing additional detail. When a diagnosis can be more precisely defined with additional characters, those specifics should be included when possible. For more details on the structure and use of these codes, one can refer to the document titled \"ICD-10-CM code structure and the conventions that govern selection.\"\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
